  <h1>Week 11 Reflection</h1>
  <h2>Graphic Novel: Motherhood in Crisis</h2>

  <p>
    By Caide Alvarez
  </p>

  <p>
   Reading “Graphic Novel: Motherhood in Crisis” by Laurence Ivil, Alicia Prager, and Saidu Bah gave me a different perspective on what motherhood can look like in difficult situations. The graphic novel format made the topic feel more personal and emotional, since the images worked together with the text to show experiences that might be hard to fully explain with words alone. It felt like I was not just reading about these situations, but actually seeing and feeling parts of them. One thing that stood out to me was how the story highlights the challenges that mothers face during times of crisis, whether those crises are related to health, poverty, or displacement. It made me realize how much strength and resilience is required to care for a child when basic needs are not always guaranteed. The visual elements, like facial expressions and settings, helped emphasize the stress and uncertainty that the mothers were going through, which made their experiences feel more real and immediate. This reading also connected to my own life in a personal way. I grew up in a single-parent household, so I have seen firsthand what it looks like for a mother to carry so much responsibility on her own. As a child, I remember noticing the stress and pressure my mom felt trying to make ends meet, even if she didn’t always say it out loud. Watching her work hard and still worry about bills or daily needs helped me understand, at a young age, how difficult it can be to balance everything. In a way, experiencing that as a child also made me more aware of her struggles and shaped how I responded to them. Because of that, I think children in these situations often develop a sense of care for their parent. Even though they may not be able to do much to actually change the situation, they still try to support in whatever small ways they can. It almost creates a role where the child feels like a kind of protector for the parent, emotionally if not physically.
  </p>

  <p>
    I can relate to that feeling, where you want to ease their stress or help carry some of the weight, even if you don’t fully understand everything at the time. This made the stories in the graphic novel feel especially real to me, even though they take place in different contexts. I also noticed how the novel doesn’t just focus on struggle, but also shows moments of care, connection, and hope. Even in very difficult conditions, the bond between mother and child is still strong. That balance made the story feel more human, rather than only sad or overwhelming. It reminded me of how, even during stressful times growing up, there were still moments where my mom made things feel normal and safe. Another important aspect was how the graphic novel format made complex global issues easier to understand. Topics like maternal health or access to resources can sometimes feel distant or abstract when read in a textbook. But in this format, they felt more relatable. Seeing individual stories helped me understand that these are not just statistics, but real people with emotions, fears, and hopes.
  </p>

  <p>
   At the same time, the reading made me think about inequality and how access to healthcare and support systems can vary so much depending on where someone lives. It made me reflect on how something as natural as motherhood can become much more dangerous or stressful in certain environments. This was a reminder of how important it is to have systems in place that support mothers and children, especially during vulnerable times. Overall, I found this graphic novel to be both informative and emotionally engaging. It helped me understand the topic of motherhood in crisis in a more personal and meaningful way. The combination of visuals and storytelling made the message stronger and easier to connect with. After reading it, I feel like I have a better awareness of the challenges some mothers face and a deeper appreciation for the strength it takes , not just globally, but also in everyday life.

  </p>

